------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7h. BOTTOM LINE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DOES TEMPORAL AVERAGING REDUCE THE NUGGET?
  Measured on IDENTICAL wells, so only the target definition changes:
  nugget share 0.205 on the single-year target, 0.198 on the mean of
  1996-2000 -- a change of -0.007.
  Independently, the within-well variance across years is 1.983 m2
  against a single-year nugget of 4.506 m2, so temporal noise can be
  at most 44% of the nugget however the variogram is fitted.
  Averaging barely moves the nugget. The short-range noise is therefore
  NOT mainly year-to-year variation: it is genuine small-scale spatial
  heterogeneity in the water table, which averaging cannot remove.

DOES IT CHANGE THE CONCLUSION?
  On identical wells, ordinary kriging RMSE moves from 3.537 m
  (single year) to 3.387 m (mean), and near-cutoff misclassification
  from 39.1% to 37.7%, against a no-information benchmark of 33.4%
  and 36.8% respectively.
  CONCLUSION UNCHANGED: even with an averaged target, interpolation does
  not beat the no-information rule near the cutoff by a usable margin.
  Step 6's finding survives temporal averaging.

HOW FUZZY IS 'BEING ABOVE 8 m' IN REALITY?
  Within a single well, the median year-to-year SD of pre-monsoon May
  depth is 0.73 m, and 19.1% of UP wells with 3+ readings record
  depths on BOTH sides of 8 m in different years. Among wells whose
  mean sits within +/-2 m of the cutoff, 54.1% straddle it.

  So the cutoff is fuzzy BEFORE any interpolation. Even a village with its
  own monitoring well ON SITE would have an ambiguous treatment status in
  a MAJORITY of near-cutoff cases (54.1%). That is a property of the water
  table, not of our method, and it caps how sharp any 8 m RD built on
  1990s CGWB readings can be -- which is a further argument for Sekhri's
  own validated assignment over a reconstruction, and for treating the
  design as fuzzy rather than sharp (PHASE0_SPEC.md 4.3).

